  geopolitics: "The transmission ran the other way this weekend for the first time in weeks, and it is the second of today's two score changes. The supply side of the Iran conflict is repairing faster than this page assumed on Friday. Saudi Arabia moved 2.8 million barrels a day through the Strait of Hormuz in the six days to September 18, against 700,000 barrels a day in August, and Aramco is now guiding to restoring full pipeline operation within six weeks; in the interim the kingdom has been clearing Ras Tanura cargoes by ship-to-ship transfer outside the strait near Oman's Sohar port, with shuttle vessels running crude through Hormuz to tankers waiting on the far side. That directly contradicts the specific claim Friday's page held CAD at 0 on - Rapidan's reading that the damaged East-West pipeline would constrain Saudi production and exports through at least end-September with risk skewed to a larger disruption - and this page marks that wrong rather than letting it age quietly. Alongside the supply repair sits a genuine lull in the fighting: Iran has held a ten-day pause in ballistic missile and drone attacks through September 19, the longest interval of the resumed war, with no fire reported into Iraq or other host states. Foreign Minister Araghchi has claimed in Beijing that Iran agreed a plan with Oman to reopen the strait, but no Omani official has confirmed it and the sticking point is unchanged and substantive - Iran wants a new access regime with charges, Oman opposes tolls and Washington calls them unacceptable - so that claim is reported here and not relied on. The United States has separately opened back-channel talks with the Houthis, whose representatives say they have no intention of attacking American vessels. Set against all of that, the transit data remains genuinely unreliable and is a reason not to over-extend the de-escalation read: Reuters recorded four transits on September 15 while Windward documented twelve on September 16, and the US Energy Secretary claimed 18 million barrels moved on September 15, a figure that reconciles to neither dataset, with the gap attributed to unreported dark-fleet activity. Prices have followed the supply news rather than the diplomacy. WTI is 98.452, through the round number, -1.84% on the day and -2.90% on the week though still +15.81% on the month; Brent is 101.909 and -3.57% on the week, roughly 55% above pre-war levels but 14% below the March peaks. The gas complex has turned with it after weeks of one-way pressure: EU gas is 78.34 EUR/MWh and -5.12% on the week, UK gas 195.24 GBp/thm and -4.85%, German gas 80.54 EUR/MWh and -0.14% - though all three remain +14% to +26% on the month, so the relief is recent and shallow rather than a trend. The FX read follows in four places. Canada loses the only leg that was holding it up and goes to -1, with USD/CAD, AUD/CAD and GBP/CAD the expressions and USOIL turning SHORT to match. Gold loses its haven leg at the same time as the Fed keeps its real-rates leg pointed the wrong way, which is why XAU/USD goes RANGE rather than SHORT - the German political result is a live haven catalyst pulling the other way and it has not been priced. The franc is the mirror image: a fading war premium is exactly what would let the SNB sound less alarmed about appreciation on Thursday, and it is why the haven bid in CHF is weakening even as a European political risk builds. And sterling and the euro both get a mild inflation reprieve from cheaper gas, which cuts against the hawkish energy case for the Bank of England specifically - the administered October cap is locked, but the Q1 2027 forecast rise is not. The asymmetry to keep in mind is that all of this reverses in minutes on a single tanker strike, which is why USOIL is SHORT at LOW-MED rather than higher and why the CAD downgrade is one notch rather than two.",

